  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The state of Oklahoma recorded $287M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs led buying activity with $242M, representing the single largest agency outlay in the state so far this fiscal year. Obligations fell 94.1% year over year, reflecting a large drop from prior fiscal years that ranged from $4.9B to $6.8B before FY2027. SIGNATURE CHOICE III, LLC is the top vendor with $226M in obligations, and Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest industry by dollars with $226M. The FY2027 totals are partial, fiscal year to date, so the $287M figure does not represent a full-year total. Oklahoma had 488 awards in FY2027 to date, with an average award size of $588K. The concentration of dollars with a single agency and a single vendor helps explain why statewide obligations are much lower this year than in prior full fiscal years, when annual totals were in the billions.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Oklahoma this fiscal year?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $242M, which makes that agency the dominant buyer in FY2027 to date. SIGNATURE CHOICE III, LLC accounted for $226M of vendor obligations, so a small number of entities capture a large share of the $287M total. Oklahoma had 488 awards and an average award size of $588K, indicating that a mix of large single vendors and smaller contracts make up the current total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How does FY2027 to date compare with recent full fiscal years?",
        "body": "FY2027 to date obligations are $287M, well below recent full-year totals of $4.9B to $6.8B in FY22 through FY26. Obligations fell 94.1% year over year, reflecting the partial-year nature of the FY2027 figure and the high concentration of prior full-year dollars. The FY2027 total should be interpreted as fiscal year to date, not a full-year amount."
      }
    ]
  },
